            <ArticleTitle>Targeting Obesity and Cachexia: Identification of the GFRAL Receptor-MIC-1/GDF15 Pathway.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1471-4914(17)30185-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.molmed.2017.10.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Macrophage inhibitory cytokine-1/growth differentiation factor 15 (MIC-1/GDF15) is a divergent transforming growth factor (TGFβ) superfamily cytokine implicated in biological and disease processes including metabolism, cancer, and chronic inflammation, but whose receptor has remained elusive. Four laboratories have recently identified GFRAL, an orphan receptor of the glial-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF) receptor α family, as the receptor for MIC-1/GDF15, signaling though the coreceptor Ret. These data identify a new systemic to central nervous system (CNS) circuit that regulates metabolism in response to stress and which could be targeted to treat both severe obesity and anorexia/cachexia syndrome.</AbstractText>
